    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Starlink seems to be working normally again in Ukraine",
      "d": "Elon Musk reportedly ordered SpaceX to throttle Ukraine's Starlink access.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/24/starlink-seems-to-be-working-normally-again-in-ukraine/",
      "m": "10. 24. PM 08:46",
      "ts": 1792874794000,
      "fullText": "In Brief Posted: 1:46 PM PDT · October 24, 2026 Image Credits: Jagmeet Singh / TechCrunch Anthony Ha Starlink seems to be working normally again in Ukraine SpaceX appears to have lifted restrictions the Ukrainian military&#8217;s of use Starshield — the military-grade version of the company&#8217;s Starlink internet system. The Wall Street Journal reports that Defense Minister Evgeniy Khmara told reporters that Starshield appears to be working normally again, although the military is still conducting tests and has not received an explanation for the restrictions. This follows a 10-day period where Ukrainian drones would lose internet connectivity when flying at speeds of around 100 miles per hour, resulting in the military losing control of some drones, while others became more vulnerable because they were flying more slowly. President Donald Trump reportedly called SpaceX CEO Elon Musk on Wednesday and asked him to restore Starshield service. Both Trump and Musk have criticized Ukraine’s President Volodymyr Zelensky, with Musk recently posting , “Zelensky has gone too far.” The WSJ previously reported that Musk ordered SpaceX to throttle Ukraine’s Starlink access, though some sources claimed he’d discussed it with the Trump administration first. Musk claimed in a post on X that Ukraine had &#8220;violated Starlink terms of service and used it for offensive drone strikes to kill people.&#8221; &#8220;Were Starlink to be turned off, the Ukrainian front line would collapse immediately,&#8221; Musk said. &#8220;This is NOT a threat, it is simply a statement of fact.&#8221; Topics Donald Trump , Elon Musk , Government & Policy , Space , SpaceX , Starlink , ukraine November 4 Boston Buy One Pass, Get a Second Free Cover more ground at TechCrunch&#8217;s ultimate founder bootcamp of the year by bringing your co-founder or partner for free Book before the BOGO deal expires Register Now Newsletters See More Subscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news TechCrunch Daily New"
